  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 407 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nA bit of a nondescript surface pressure pattern sits over the \nWFO MPX coverage area with some scattered showers/thunderstorms \nearly this morning over far eastern MN through western WI. A few\nmeager troughs at the surface can be analyzed while the main \nsurface low lingers over eastern SD/NE. Aloft, the large trough \nemanating from Hudson Bay continues to rotate several shortwave \ndisturbances around its western and southern fringes through the\nUpper Midwest. The most important feature is LLJ in between \n900-700mb, helping spark the thunderstorm activity within an \nenvironment sporting a roughly 1.50\" PWAT. The \nmoisture/instability gradient shows up nicely at the surface \nwith dewpoints into the low- mid 60s in western MN while \ndewpoints in the mid 50s to around 60 are evident in eastern MN \nto western WI.\n\nThe expectation is that going towards sunrise, the push of the \nsurface low into IA within the elevated moisture/unstable \nenvironment will help fill in the area over southwestern-\nsouthern MN with additional showers/thunderstorms. Not \nnecessarily expecting much in the way of strong-severe \nthunderstorms but the storms ongoing overnight have generally \nproduced 0.25\"-0.50\" hail along with frequent lightning and \nrainfall rates nearing 1 in/hr. The storms are progressive to \nthe east, thus mitigating flooding concerns thus far. However, \nshould storm coverage overachieve, even FFG over 2 in/hr could \nbe realized in pockets.\n\nStorms are expected to wane in coverage/intensity from late\nmorning into the early afternoon, with partial clearing \nthereafter as the low pressure center and its associated fronts \nmove to the E and SE of the coverage area. This will then allow \nhigh pressure to nudge southward from Canada. While this \nscenario will not bring in an airmass change in terms of \ntemperatures/dewpoints, it will push the lingering frontal \nboundary far south enough to allow for dry weather to prevail \nfrom this afternoon through Monday evening. The front does look \nto retreat back north just enough to spawn another round of \nscattered showers/thunderstorms early morning Tuesday, in \nconjunction with one last gasp from the upper low over eastern \nNOAM. Like this morning, there is the possibility of a few \nstronger storms but organization is again lacking as is diurnal \nassistance due to the overnight timing.\n\nThe rest of the week looks to run mainly dry aside from an\nisolated chance here or there of a few showers. The topic then\nchanges to one of temperatures as temperatures will remain \ngenerally close to normal through Wednesday with highs in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s, possibly as high as 84-85 degrees. The \nupper ridge over the western CONUS is expected to flatten/shift \nsouthward slightly while the NOAM trough shifts to the eastern \nseaboard, making for more pronounced northwest flow aloft for \nthe north-central CONUS. Cooler western Canadian high pressure \nwill slide southeast to the Upper Midwest, bringing a cooler \nairmass to our region from Thursday onward. This will push highs\ninto the 70s area-wide for the latter portion of the week, with\nlows into the 50s away from the Twin Cities metro (and near 60 \nwithin the TC metro).",
